## Hypothesis ID and title

**H-003 — SSR/SSG vs. CSR content availability**

## Research question

How do CSR, SSR, and SSG affect meaningful content availability to crawlers and retrieval systems?

## Hypothesis statement

When equivalent content is present in the initial HTML response, SSR and SSG may make that content available to clients that do not execute page JavaScript more consistently than a CSR variant that requires JavaScript execution.

## Theoretical background

Rendering strategies differ in when HTML content is produced. Actual crawler behavior depends on the client, execution policy, timing, and indexing pipeline.

## Supporting literature and existing evidence

Not yet assessed; no evidence or source records are linked.

## Expected outcome

The initial-response HTML and controlled no-JavaScript extraction measures are expected to distinguish variants; crawler and retrieval outcomes remain to be tested.

## Alternative explanations and potential counterarguments

Some crawlers execute JavaScript; SSR/SSG pages can still fail due to access, errors, or timing. Hydration, caching, network behavior, and content parity can confound results.

## Validation approach

Use content parity checks, inspect response HTML and rendered output, record crawler/tool configurations, and follow [EXP-002](/en/05-experiments/exp-002/).

## Related experiments and findings

- Proposed: EXP-002.
- Findings: none recorded.
